Commentary of al-Nasafi (Madarik al-Tanzil) on surah 35, verse 24:

﴿Indeed, We have sent you with the truth﴾. "With the truth" is a circumstantial phrase (ḥāl) referring to one of the two pronouns (in "sent you" and "We"), meaning: "as one who is truthful (muḥiqqan)," or "as ones who are truthful (muḥiqqīn)." Alternatively, it is an attributive modifier of the verbal noun (ṣifah li-l-maṣdar), meaning: a sending accompanied by the truth.

﴿A bringer of good tidings﴾, with the promise;

﴿and a warner﴾, with the threat;

﴿And there is no nation﴾: "And there is no nation before your nation." And "ummah" (nation) means a large group;

﴿but a warner had passed among them﴾ [al-Qasas: 23]. The word "ummah" is also used for the people of every age, and what is meant here is the people of the era. The traces of warning remained between ʿĪsā (Jesus) and Muḥammad, peace be upon them both, so those nations were never without a warner. When the traces of ʿĪsā's warning (peace be upon him) were effaced, Muḥammad (ﷺ) was sent.

﴿Except that there passed among them﴾: went on;

﴿a warner﴾, who warns them, especially against tyranny and the evil end of disbelief. He suffices with mention of "warner" instead of "bringer of good tidings" at the end of the verse, having mentioned both, because warning is accompanied by good tidings, so mention of warning indicates mention of good tidings.

﴿And they will say: "Praise be to Allah, who has removed from us all grief,"﴾ — the fear of the Fire, or the fear of death, or the cares of this world;

﴿Indeed, our Lord is All-Forgiving,﴾ who forgives sins, however many they may be;

﴿All-Appreciative.﴾ who accepts acts of obedience, however small they may be.
